EXAMPLE: Stepper on a step/dir driver (A4988, DRV8825, TMC2209) with AccelStepper (Arduino (C++)) — STEP, DIR and ENABLE pins with acceleration, back and forth one turn; wiring, current limit and microsteps explained.
language arduino
// A stepper on a step/dir driver (A4988, DRV8825, TMC2208/TMC2209 in step/dir
// mode) with AccelStepper: smooth acceleration, back and forth 800 steps.
// Wiring: STEP -> pin 3, DIR -> pin 2, EN -> pin 4 (LOW = enabled),
//         driver VMOT to the motor supply (12-24 V, 100 uF across it), GND shared.
// Set the driver's current limit (Vref) for your motor before running it.
// Steps per turn = 200 (a 1.8 degree motor) x microsteps (MS pins / driver default).
// This sketch moves 800 steps forward, then 800 back, repeating.
#include <AccelStepper.h>

const int stepPin = 3;
const int dirPin = 2;
const int enablePin = 4;
const long stepsPerMove = 800;   // Move 800 steps in each direction

AccelStepper stepper(AccelStepper::DRIVER, stepPin, dirPin);

void setup() {
  pinMode(enablePin, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(enablePin, LOW);       // enable the driver
  stepper.setMaxSpeed(1000);          // steps per second (adjust as needed)
  stepper.setAcceleration(500);       // steps per second per second (smooth accel)
  stepper.moveTo(stepsPerMove);       // start by moving forward 800 steps
}

void loop() {
  stepper.run();   // call as often as possible: never delay() here

  // If we have reached the target position, reverse direction
  if (stepper.distanceToGo() == 0) {
    stepper.moveTo(-stepper.currentPosition());
  }
}
END

